A||N||8||fiero651||Broken Exhaust manifold bolts||||9||
Z||000000||fiero651||06-07-2001||01:06 AM||||Yeh everyone I have got a big problem.  Well I have at least one manifold bolts broken on each side. with half the bolt still in the head.  The worst of course is the one on the firewall side you can't really see it but you can feel it.  What can I do to get these broken bolts out of the heads?||||reg||9
Z||000001||tgowens||06-07-2001||04:17 AM||||the best way is to use an "easy-out" but, be sure to drill square into the center of the bolt........drill a small pilot hole for the easy out first otherwise you may slide off and find that you are drilling into the block beside the bolt rather than into the broken bolt itself.....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||09:15 AM||||Its not all as simple as replaceing the bolts BTW.<P>  to get to the back one I am pretty sure you have to drop the motor.<P>  but the real issue is not the bolt, but the gasket - if the bolt has been missing for a while the gasket is most likely burned/blown away from that part of the exhaust manifold. (someone correct me here if I am wrong).<P>   so replacing the bolt wont stop the manifold from leaking, your gonna have to replace the gasket.<P>  which means you have to take all the rest of the bolts out too. How many of those will also snap off in the process?!<P>  How bad do you want to stop the leak?||||reg||11
Z||000003||86ttops||06-07-2001||10:33 AM||||Yeh I think i will have to drop the motor the gasket has been replaced once since the bolt broke off.  The manifold on the trunk side has 2 boltsd broke off! I haven't touched this one yet.  I have a new gasket for it but I am not gonna touch it till I get these bolts out of there.||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadaver||06-07-2001||11:38 PM||||YOU DONT NEED TO DROP THE MOTOR. I have drills that you can do it with. Not sure how to get them to you and I dont mean to sound like an ass but they are expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||08:51 AM||||you mean drill bits or the drill itself (motor and chuck)<P>is it small or shaped oddly so you can get at the bolts with it? Pnumatic?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rodney||06-08-2001||09:18 AM||||I have one of the original Kent Tools(?)fixtures. I have been looking to make copies. It bolts to the block and you drill out the bad bolt(s) using it as a drill jig. It ensures you stay in the center of the broken bolt with the drill bit. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Toddster||06-08-2001||08:00 PM||||With all due respect you're getting bad advice.<P>I've had this problem and I own 7 Fieros so pay attention:<P>1) place the back of the car on a set of jack stands and chock the front wheels.<P>2) Use a good floor jack to let the engine K-frame swing out of the way and hang on the Dog-Bone after removing the two rear bolts. It is that simple, it takes two minutes.<P>3) This makes it easy to get to the exhaust manifold on the firewall side from above. Just sit in the trunk and remove the manifold (I've gotten to the point where I can swap a set of manifolds in 3 hours). DON'T USE AN EASY OUT. First, how are you going to get a drill in there? And second, they are not Easy. You will destroy the one chance you have of removing the bolt. After the manifold is out of the way, use plently of WD-40 and a good old fashion pair of vise grips to crank it out. Clean the bolt hole threads with a tap after the bolt is removed.<P>4) replace the bolts with a set of grade 12 bolts and a LIGHT dabbing of lock-tite and you will never have a broken bolt again.<P>5) this is a good time to use a die grinder to take out the flashing in the exhaust manifolds. It will give you an extra 9 hp and eliminate the problem of cracking.<P>Feel free to send me an email for more details.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ka4nkf||06-08-2001||10:40 PM||||Try this first. I had one broken off on number 5 cyl. After I got the manifold off I could not see the stud enough to drill a pilot hole. So I put a mirrow below and centered punched the stud (was not easy) but I got lucky. then I drilled a pilot hole using the mirrow to see. Ok this is the strange part, as I was drilling I noticed the stud turned in toward the block, so I said Hecht that stud is not frozen. so I took my drill bit and put it back into the hole and I started turning the opposite direction and the stud came out. What I am saying stick a small screw driver in first and maybe the stud will turn out. All these broken studs are not broken off by a wrench.I know all of mine were ok but it broke on its own. I guess from over tighten it.Try this and you may get lucky||||reg||1
